"The first job of the skeleton is support. Your spine, a stack of 24 small bones called vertebrae resting on a larger bone called the sacrum, holds up your head and body like the pole of a tent. Your pelvis passes that weight to your legs, and your legs carry it down to your feet. The second job is protection. Your skull is a hard helmet around your brain. Your ribs form a cage around your heart and lungs, and your vertebrae make a bony tunnel that shields your spinal cord, the bundle of nerves that carries messages between your brain and the rest of your body.": "bda748231eb4",
"The fourth and fifth jobs are hidden inside. Many bones are filled with a soft tissue called marrow. Red marrow, which in adults is found mostly in flat bones such as the ribs, hips, and vertebrae, makes new blood cells, hundreds of billions of them every day. They include red blood cells that carry oxygen, white blood cells that fight germs, and platelets that help a cut stop bleeding. Bones also store minerals, especially calcium. When your heart and nerves need more calcium than your food provides, your bones release some into your blood.": "a997f886d511",
